<h2 id="architecture">Two parts: a service and a dashboard</h2>
<p>IIS Monitor has two programs, both on the IIS server. A Windows service does all the collecting, and the dashboard shows what it collects.</p>

<div class="diagram" role="img" aria-label="Diagram: the IIS Monitor service reads IIS configuration, process data, performance counters, ETW traces, the TCP table and SQL Server; it writes history to a local SQLite database and sends live snapshots over a local named pipe to the dashboard.">
    <div class="diagram-row">
        <div class="diagram-node"><strong>IIS configuration</strong><span>sites, pools, bindings</span></div>
        <div class="diagram-node"><strong>Processes</strong><span>w3wp.exe and children</span></div>
        <div class="diagram-node"><strong>IIS counters</strong><span>requests, queues</span></div>
        <div class="diagram-node"><strong>ETW traces</strong><span>kernel I/O, IIS log</span></div>
        <div class="diagram-node"><strong>TCP table, SQL Server</strong><span>connections, DMVs</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="diagram-arrow">every update (0.25 s – 1 min)</div>
    <div class="diagram-row">
        <div class="diagram-node primary"><strong>IIS Monitor service</strong><span>Windows service, LocalSystem, always running</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="diagram-arrow">history and live snapshots</div>
    <div class="diagram-row">
        <div class="diagram-node"><strong>history.db</strong><span>SQLite in %ProgramData%\IISMonitor</span></div>
        <div class="diagram-node"><strong>Named pipe</strong><span>local, Administrators and SYSTEM only</span></div>
        <div class="diagram-node primary"><strong>IIS Monitor dashboard</strong><span>IISMonitor.exe: live views, history, settings</span></div>
    </div>
</div>

<dl class="defs">
    <dt>The service</dt>
    <dd><code>IISMonitor</code> runs as LocalSystem and starts with Windows. On every update it reads the IIS configuration, the process table, performance counters, two ETW trace sessions, the TCP table and, if configured, SQL Server. It writes history to <code>%ProgramData%\IISMonitor\history.db</code>, runs the auto blacklist, and streams live snapshots to the dashboard.</dd>
    <dt>The dashboard</dt>
    <dd><code>IISMonitor.exe</code> is a Windows desktop app. It connects to the service through a local named pipe that only Administrators and SYSTEM can open. If the service isn't running, it runs the same collector itself (<a href="/docs/getting-started#standalone">standalone mode</a>) so the live view still works.</dd>
</dl>

