<h2 id="worth-knowing">Things worth knowing</h2>
<h3 id="pool-not-site">CPU, memory, disk and database connections belong to an app pool</h3>
<p>IIS runs each application pool in its own processes. If several sites share one pool, those numbers can't be split between them. Requests, bandwidth and response times are still per site. One pool per site gives the clearest picture.</p>

<h3 id="http-sys">Website traffic doesn't go through w3wp.exe</h3>
<p>The kernel driver HTTP.sys owns ports 80 and 443, so Windows charges incoming website traffic to the System process. That is why website traffic comes from IIS's counters. A pool's network total adds its sites' website traffic, credited to the pool running each site's root application, to the outbound traffic its own processes create. Per-process numbers are outbound only, and loopback traffic between a pool's own processes isn't counted twice.</p>

<h3 id="etw">Live response times come from IIS's ETW log target</h3>
<p>IIS can write its W3C log to files, to ETW, or both. <span class="ui">Enable response times</span> sets the target to <code>File, ETW</code> and turns on the fields <code>s-sitename</code>, <code>cs-uri-stem</code>, <code>sc-status</code> and <code>time-taken</code> if they are off. Log files are written as before. This needs the HTTP Logging role service; sites using central binary logging can't be traced.</p>

<h3 id="tcp">Counting SQL Server connections over TCP sees network connections</h3>
<p>If an app talks to a SQL Server on the same machine over shared memory or named pipes, there is no TCP connection to count. Add a connection string in Settings: SQL Server then reports the sessions itself, active and idle, and the Database tab lights up.</p>

<h3 id="cpu-percent">CPU % is a share of the whole machine</h3>
<p>Like Task Manager's Processes tab, 100 % means every core is busy. A single-threaded loop on a 16-core server shows about 6 %.</p>

<h3 id="recycles">Idle pools and recycles are handled</h3>
<p>Pools without a worker process yet show 0 processes, not an error. Process IDs change on every recycle; IIS Monitor re-maps them on every update.</p>

<h2 id="footprint">A light footprint</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Everything it reads already exists in Windows and IIS: process counters, performance counters and ETW events. Nothing is injected into your applications.</li>
    <li>SQL Server is asked one light query per sample; nothing is installed there and no trace is started.</li>
    <li>Nothing listens on the network; the dashboard and service talk over a local named pipe.</li>
    <li>Kernel and IIS tracing can each be switched off in <a href="/features/settings#tracing">Settings</a>.</li>
</ul>
